    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is an aggressive hematologic malignancy that results from accumulation of mutations within hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs). Approximately 15% of AML patients carry mutations in the signaling protein NRAS. The most common NRAS mutation in AML, the G12D substitution, results in upregulated cell proliferation. Although the role of NRAS in hematologic malignancies has been studied in mice models, there are no studies investigating the effects of endogenous oncogenic NRAS mutations on human HSPCs. So, the purpose of this research was to develop a human HSPC model that is more representative of AML in patients to study how NRAS mutations contribute to leukemogenesis. For this HSPC model, a CRISPR/Cas9 knock-in system was generated to precisely introduce the NRAS-G12D mutant cDNA into either one or both alleles of the endogenous NRAS locus. The effects of both heterozygous and homozygous NRAS-G12D mutations on human HSPCs were characterized by analyzing cell proliferation, replating potential, and differentiation patterns. HSPCs with homozygous NRAS-G12D mutations had the highest initial proliferation rate, while heterozygous mutant cells retained a steady proliferative advantage. Similarly, homozygous mutant HSPCs initially had higher colony forming ability, but heterozygous mutant HSPCs had the highest replating potential. In parallel to these trends, NRAS-G12D/G12D HSPCs exhibited only short-term skewing of granulo-monocytic differentiation towards monocytes, while differentiation of NRAS-WT/G12D HSPCs showed long-term monocytic skewing. These results support clinical observations that heterozygous NRAS mutations in AML give rise to subpopulations of HSPCs with sustained increases in proliferation and monocytic differentiation. Ultimately, improved understanding of how NRAS mutations contribute to leukemogenesis can provide insight into potential therapeutic targets for patients with NRAS-mutant AML.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Choi, S., Collins, C., and Majeti, R. (2024). Investigating the effects of NRAS-G12D mutant gene dosage in the pathogenesis of acute myeloid leukemia using CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing in human HSPCs. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/tb617js2561. https://doi.org/10.25740/tb617js2561.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
